3. Loop Invariant Code Motion (LICM): This optimization technique moves loop-invariant expressions out of the loop. For example, in the following code, the value of `i` is the same for all iterations of the loop, so it can be computed before the loop.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i, j, k;
    i = 5;
    for (j = 0; j < 10; j++) {
        k = i + j;
        printf("k = %d\n", k);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

4. Common Subexpression Elimination (CSE): This optimization technique eliminates redundant computations of the same expression. For example, in the following code, the value of `i * i` is computed twice.

```c
